= Choosing Our Enemies

In our agitation, press, and campaign materials, who do we name as the principal enemy of the Brazilian people?

[? if enemies != 0 : Previously, we had identified the [+ enemies +] as our main target. Has the situation changed enough to warrant a new line? ?]

- @anti_dem: The enemies of democracy on all sides — the right-wing conspirators and the ultra-left adventurists alike.
- @latifundia: The latifundiários and the landed oligarchy.
- @imperialism: North American imperialism and the CIA.
- @udn: The UDN and its military allies specifically.
- @pcb: The PCB — the revisionists who have abandoned Marxism-Leninism.
- @national_bourgeoisie: The national bourgeoisie and their political representatives.
- @easy_discard

@anti_dem
on-arrival: enemies = "enemies of democracy"; moderate_strength += 5; reformist_strength += 5; combative_dissent += 5; workers_pcdo -= 2 if workers_udn >= 5; coup_progress -= 1 if coup_progress > 0; pcb_relation -= 10

Presenting ourselves as defenders of constitutional order against extremism of all kinds appeals to the moderate middle and makes us more palatable to progressive nationalists and Goulart's wing of the PTB. However, the combative tendency bristles — lumping the ultra-left in with the coup plotters feels like a betrayal of revolutionary solidarity, and it will make any future reconciliation with dissident leftists harder.

@latifundia
on-arrival: enemies = "latifundiários and the landed oligarchy"; combative_strength += 5; combative_dissent -= 5; peasants_pcdo += 3*(1-dissent); workers_udn -= 3; udn_relation -= 5; moderate_dissent += 3

Naming the latifundiary class as the root of Brazil's misery resonates powerfully in the countryside and gives the Peasant Leagues a clear class enemy to organize around. The moderate tendency worries this will alarm the rural middle — the small landowners whose support we need — but the combative faction considers it the most honest statement of the party's analysis.

@imperialism
on-arrival: enemies = "North American imperialism"; nationalist_line += 1; combative_dissent -= 5; students_pcdo += 3*(1-dissent); military_pcdo += 2*(1-dissent) if nationalist_line >= 1; pcb_relation -= 5; coup_progress += 1

Anti-imperialism is our broadest possible rallying cry — it unites communists, nationalists, progressive military officers, and students under a single banner, and it puts us in step with the global tide of national liberation movements. The CIA will take notice. Naming Washington so directly may accelerate the timeline of reaction, but it also sharpens the contradictions we need the masses to understand.

@udn
on-arrival: enemies = "UDN and its military allies"; combative_strength += 5; coup_urgency += 1; workers_pcdo += 2*(1-dissent); pro_democracy += 3*(1-dissent); pcb_relation += 5; moderate_dissent += 5

Targeting the UDN specifically — Lacerda, the Ipes, the ESG network — names the actual conspirators rather than abstract social forces. This is popular across most of the party and makes common cause with the PCB easier, since we share the same immediate enemy. The moderate tendency worries that it makes us appear as a purely defensive force rather than a revolutionary one.

@pcb
on-arrival: enemies = "PCB revisionists"; combative_strength += 8; combative_dissent -= 5; pcb_relation -= 20; moderate_dissent += 10; students_pcdo += 2*(1-dissent) if sino_soviet_split >= 1; coup_progress += 1

[? if sino_soviet_split < 1 : The split from the PCB is still fresh and the wounds are raw, but openly campaigning against them as revisionists risks fracturing the left at the very moment unity is most needed. Many in our party are uneasy with this choice. ?][? if sino_soviet_split >= 1 : The Sino-Soviet split has made the ideological lines undeniable. The PCB has chosen Moscow and peaceful coexistence — we name this for what it is: a capitulation to reformism dressed up in Marxist language. ?]

@national_bourgeoisie
on-arrival: enemies = "national bourgeoisie"; combative_strength += 8; combative_dissent -= 5; reformist_dissent += 10; moderate_dissent += 8; pcb_relation -= 10; coup_progress += 2; workers_pcdo += 3*(1-dissent)

Attacking the national bourgeoisie directly breaks sharply from the democratic-front line that has defined much of the Brazilian left since 1945. The moderates are alarmed — this stance puts us at odds with Goulart and the PTB, whose base includes precisely this class. But the combative tendency argues that the national bourgeoisie has already shown it will choose order over reform, and that any strategy built on their support is built on sand.